WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.089
Vsauce!

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:02.089
Kevin here.

00:00:02.089 --> 00:00:05.670
And you are lying to yourself… basically
all day long.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:06.860
You don’t even know it!

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:12.370
And sometimes other people are conspiring
to steer your mind where it shouldn’t go.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:14.080
You get patterns wrong.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.570
You get numbers wrong.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.980
You get prices and values wrong.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.199
And it’s totally not your fault.

00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:21.199
Right?

00:00:21.199 --> 00:00:22.199
Wrong!

00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:27.670
It’s why 40,320 is actually greater than
40,320.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.830
Wait, huh?

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:40.280
Ok, I’ve got two very simple math problems
here under these papers, A and B. Choose the

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.860
one you want to solve.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:43.860
Choose right now.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.570
Do you wanna solve A or do you wanna solve
B?

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:47.790
Go ahead.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:52.040
Okay now that you’ve chosen, when I remove
the papers to expose the problems, you’ll

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.600
have exactly 5 seconds to solve the one you
chose.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.940
And if you don’t have enough time to solve
it, you can just estimate the answer.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.090
Ready?

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:02.230
GO.

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.519
TIME’S UP.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:10.920
Alright, let’s be honest -- virtually no
one was able to multiply those numbers fast

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.679
enough, at least not accurately.

00:01:12.679 --> 00:01:14.639
But what about your estimates?

00:01:14.639 --> 00:01:24.139
We know that 1x2x3x4x5x6x7x8 and 8x7x6x5x4x3x2x1
are the exact same problem.

00:01:24.139 --> 00:01:29.639
Because of the commutative property, which
is like a times b equals b times a, it doesn’t

00:01:29.639 --> 00:01:33.280
matter what order we multiply the terms.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.069
Start small, get bigger?

00:01:35.069 --> 00:01:36.459
Start big, get smaller?

00:01:36.459 --> 00:01:39.229
It makes no difference, same result.

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:45.109
But in a 1974 article called Judgment Under
Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biases, Amos Tversky

00:01:45.109 --> 00:01:49.840
and Daniel Kahneman revealed that the group
who tried to solve Problem A -- multiplying

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:55.599
with the small numbers first -- had a median
estimate of 512.

00:01:55.599 --> 00:01:59.689
Group B, who started by multiplying larger
descending numbers?

00:01:59.689 --> 00:02:06.679
Their median estimate was 2,250 -- over 4x
higher than A.

00:02:06.679 --> 00:02:09.759
Just because the order of the numbers was
different?

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:11.189
That seems weird!

00:02:11.189 --> 00:02:12.970
No, that’s normal.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:15.459
We do this all the time and just don’t know
it!

00:02:15.459 --> 00:02:21.769
Oh, also, the actual answer to this problem
is, as you may have guessed, 40,320.

00:02:21.769 --> 00:02:28.689
So both groups were way off each other and
way, way off the truth.

00:02:28.689 --> 00:02:35.230
The fact is: whether you started with big
numbers or small numbers, you were anchored

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:39.840
into a mode of thought that depended on your
initial information.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.780
Anchoring is a cognitive bias that basically
sets the tone for how you think about something…

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:51.549
and it can be really tough to break out of
it or consider other relevant factors.

00:02:51.549 --> 00:02:53.829
Like video game prices.

00:02:53.829 --> 00:03:01.049
I recently got into a teeny tiny Twitter argument
about how $70 games now are actually cheaper

00:03:01.049 --> 00:03:04.209
than the prices of video games 20 years ago.

00:03:04.209 --> 00:03:11.939
Paying $60 bucks in the year 2000 was actually
$90.69 in real dollars.

00:03:11.939 --> 00:03:17.870
But we’ve all been anchored into video game
pricing -- even ignoring inflation, we didn’t

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:24.019
talk about how games also now take longer
to develop, with way more staff, and the technology

00:03:24.019 --> 00:03:29.609
itself is a lot better with 4K graphics and
smooth framerates, wireless controllers, full

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:34.799
games you can buy, download and play basically
right away from your couch without having

00:03:34.799 --> 00:03:39.610
to beg your mom to drive you to KBToys at
the Southside Mall in Oneonta an hour round

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:44.530
trip in a sudden snowstorm to get a game you
know next to nothing about aside from having

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:49.310
drooled over the same 10 screenshots in GamePro
magazine for the last six months.

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:54.620
But that game was Xenogears and it was totally
worth it!

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:57.549
And anchoring happens with stuff like movies.

00:03:57.549 --> 00:04:04.830
How a movie opens and ends -- bookend anchoring
-- has a stronger influence on what you think

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:05.830
of it.

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:11.049
Have you ever heard someone say, “Yeah,
the beginning and end of that movie were terrible,

00:04:11.049 --> 00:04:13.779
but the middle was really good!”

00:04:13.779 --> 00:04:14.779
No.

00:04:14.779 --> 00:04:15.779
COIN FLIPPING.

00:04:15.779 --> 00:04:19.720
Massimo Piatelli-Palmarini flipped a coin
7 times.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:23.490
And the outcome was one of these three sequences:

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:28.710
Heads Heads Heads Heads Tails Tails Tails
Tails Heads Heads Tails Heads Tails Tails

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:31.389
Tails Tails Tails Tails Tails Tails Tails

00:04:31.389 --> 00:04:32.389
The question is.

00:04:32.389 --> 00:04:35.350
which one was most likely to be the real outcome?

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:41.020
Let’s say you had to bet on it -- it cost
you $10 if you were wrong and you were right

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:42.490
you won $30.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:49.750
When a group was polled, the top choice was
sequence 2, followed by 1, and then 3 -- for

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:51.040
all the wrong reasons.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:57.560
The probabilities of all three sequences are
exactly the same, because each coin flip is

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:02.360
an independent event with 50/50 odds of heads
or tails.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:10.200
7 tails in a row is just as likely to occur
as the mix of T’s and H’s in the 2nd series

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:14.199
-- but since we’re anchored into thinking
there’s typically an alternating mix of

00:05:14.199 --> 00:05:22.380
heads and tails in a sequence of 7 coin tosses,
we wrongly believe it’s actually more likely.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:23.699
EHHHHH.

00:05:23.699 --> 00:05:25.020
DICE.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:31.069
I’ve painted 4 sides of this die green and
the other two sides red.

00:05:31.069 --> 00:05:33.009
Same betting terms as the coin flip game..

00:05:33.009 --> 00:05:36.639
Lose $10 if you’re wrong, win $30 if you’re
right.

00:05:36.639 --> 00:05:38.910
Which outcome is more likely?

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:43.699
Red Green Red Red Red
Green Red Green Red Red Red

00:05:43.699 --> 00:05:45.729
Green Red Red Red Red Red

00:05:45.729 --> 00:05:54.000
When people were polled, choice preferences
also went 2, 1, and 3… and that is weird.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:01.259
None of the 3 sequences are that common because
they’re so red-heavy, and red is only 2

00:06:01.259 --> 00:06:03.599
of the 6 sides of the die.

00:06:03.599 --> 00:06:09.600
But our cognitive bias tells us to choose
the 2nd sequence because it’s got a nice

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.080
little mix…I don’t know why..

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:17.629
I don’t know why I did that… it’s got
a nice little mix of red and green… even

00:06:17.629 --> 00:06:23.260
though the first sequence is actually contained
in the second one.

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:24.260
Look at this!

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:26.060
Red green red red red.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:27.430
Red green red red red.

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:34.190
Why would it be easier to add an additional
throw to make the sequence more likely?

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:35.389
It isn’t!

00:06:35.389 --> 00:06:40.690
The more throws you make, the less likely
you are to hit that exact sequence.

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:42.300
HERE’S THE MATH.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:49.389
We’re just multiplying the odds of 1/3 for
red and 2/3 for green -- but check out the

00:06:49.389 --> 00:06:51.349
relative probabilities.

00:06:51.349 --> 00:06:58.500
Just one swap from red to green in sequence
#2 DOUBLES the probability of sequence #3…

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:06.220
and not requiring that extra green throw in
#1 makes it about 60% more likely to happen

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:09.020
compared to sequence #2.

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:10.520
ANCHORING COSTS YOU REAL MONEY.

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:14.240
The next time you’re shopping and you see
something that’s $19.99?

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:20.050
Yeah, $20 sounds a lot more expensive even
though it’s just a penny difference -- but

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:27.490
that $0.99 also anchors you into thinking
about the price in an interval of PENNIES,

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:31.320
so sales of WHOLE DOLLARS off seem more appealing.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:32.320
Look.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:36.330
If a car’s sticker price is $18,000 even?

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:38.919
You might counter with $15,000 even.

00:07:38.919 --> 00:07:48.919
But a car at $17,800 anchors you into countering
in hundreds, not thousands.

00:07:48.919 --> 00:07:54.099
I began this video with that multiplication
example because it's the clearest illustration

00:07:54.099 --> 00:08:00.889
of how anchoring bias can lead two groups,
with the same information just presented differently,

00:08:00.889 --> 00:08:08.270
to reach totally different conclusions, BOTH
of which were dramatically wrong.

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:09.909
Anchoring is a good thing.

00:08:09.909 --> 00:08:11.050
It’s a first impression.

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:16.400
We have to start somewhere if we’re ever
going to process any information in a meaningful

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:18.210
or efficient way.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:22.120
But it can definitely lead you down the wrong
path.

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:29.400
And without recognizing what it is and isn’t
-- you just have no hope of overcoming its

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:30.460
effects.

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:31.520
But.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:38.110
By acknowledging anchoring bias you’re armed
with the ability to choose how much weight

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:39.110
to give it.

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:44.401
You can stick with the good, accurate anchors
that lead you down the right roads and you

00:08:44.401 --> 00:08:51.920
can transcend those that otherwise would have
caused you to get it really wrong.

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:59.860
The truth is… that depending on the question,
the real right answer might still be floating

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:08.050
around waiting for someone, someone like you,
to finally anchor it.

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:10.670
And as always - thanks for watching.

